Syntax: distill <item>

Distill destroys a piece of jewelry and draws what it carried into essence.

Only jewelry.  A shaman's bangle is how anything else becomes distillable,
which is why the two skills stand together.

Every stat on the piece deposits, not one chosen from among them: a ring of
strength and dexterity yields both.  How much depends on the size of the
stat and on how well you know the work, and it is never nothing.

What you deposit goes into an essence of that stat you already carry, if you
have one, rather than making a second.  That is what lets essence grow at
all -- charges scattered across duplicates never reach anything.

Armor is not among the stats an essence can hold.  Armor belongs to the
body, and to the smiths.

THE PRICE

The work is done over a body.  You need a scalpel in hand and forceps in the
other, an operating table in the room, and a corpse that answered to the
virtue you are drawing out.

The greatest stat on the piece is the one that names the corpse -- not the
first written on it, the largest.  Its class must be one that carries that
stat in life:

  strength      warrior or monk
  brawn         warrior, deikhan or shaman
  constitution  warrior, deikhan or cleric
  dexterity     thief, shaman or monk
  agility       warrior or monk
  speed         thief or monk
  intelligence  mage or shaman
  wisdom        deikhan or cleric
  focus         thief, mage or shaman
  perception    cleric or mage
  charisma      deikhan, thief or mage
  karma         cleric or shaman

The corpse must also have been strong enough to be worth drawing from.
Jewelry answers for half of what it carries, so five levels of the dead buy
one point of the stat: a ring of ten dexterity wants a level fifty thief,
shaman or monk.  A piece carrying nothing but hit points, mana or movement
names no corpse and costs none.

The body may be in your arms or at your feet, and it is spent only when the
work finishes -- breaking off costs you nothing but the time already in it.
A corpse rots, so a shaman's embalming is what makes one found today worth
keeping until the piece to spend it on turns up.

